# .tradzhignore - files and directories the checker skips.
#
# Why this is needed: some files legitimately contain both scripts and must not
# be reported.
#   - the glyph tables ARE lists of variant-specific characters
#   - zh-cn locale files are Simplified by design
#   - build output vendors third-party code
#
# Syntax: one glob per line, # starts a comment, blank lines ignored.
# Patterns match against the full path and the bare file name.

# the checker's own data tables (these are lists OF the very glyphs we look for)
**/simplified-only.json
**/traditional-only.json
**/traditional-to-simplified.json
**/simplified-to-traditional.json
**/cantonese-only.json
**/japanese-only.json
**/tc-vocabulary.json
**/sc-vocabulary.json
**/cjk-compatibility.json
**/glyph-preferences.json
# The codepage repertoires list the characters cp950 / cp936 / cp932 / cp1252 / cp20936 CAN
# encode, which includes Simplified ones. Without this line every check reports thousands of
# hits - see references/console-encoding-hazard.md.
**/codepage-repertoire.json

# conversion test fixtures: the Simplified -> Traditional inputs ARE Simplified
**/selftest-cases.json

# this project's own dev probes: their probe data IS Simplified, like the fixtures above.
# Scoped to the project's own directory name on purpose - a bare `**/dev/**` would ship a
# rule that silently skips a USER's dev folder, and this file must stay project-neutral.
**/chinese-script-policy/dev/**

# software localization bundles: each locale is written in its own script
**/public/locales/**
**/locales/**
**/lang/**

# build output and vendored code
**/dist/**
**/build/**
**/node_modules/**
**/.git/**

# Directories that are specific to one project (a vendored upstream checkout, a
# local model folder, ...) belong in that project's OWN .tradzhignore, not here:
# this file ships with the tool and must stay project-neutral.
